{#-
  The type mapping of the Node.js worked example, and the naming and quoting
  rules that go with it.

  FR-ENV-009 and FR-ENV-010 are why this is not a filter of `tpl`, and
  BR-ENV-002 is why it never will be: a type mapping is an opinion, and an
  opinion belongs to the project that holds it. FR-ENV-011 fixes the form the
  opinion takes — a template macro — and FR-EX-008 fixes its obligation: a
  JavaScript type for every value of `data_type` the model reports for a column
  of `sakila`, `world` and `freight`. There are 39 of them, and all 39 are
  branched on below.

  Nothing falls through. The final branch calls `fail()`, per FR-ENV-020 and
  FR-SEM-014, so a type this file has never met ends the render with 65 and
  names the column, rather than reaching the type checker as a plausible guess.

  The mapping reads `data_type` through the `sql_type` filter of FR-ENV-039.

  Every mapping below was measured against the server of FR-EX-006: a real
  non-NULL value of a real column of each of the 39 types was read through
  mysql2 3.24.4 and its runtime type recorded. Nothing here was inferred from
  the driver's documentation, and the three the measurement settled against the
  obvious guess are BIGINT, the spatial types and BIT.
-#}

{#-
  value_type(column) — the JavaScript type of a column's value, nullability
  aside.

  The opinions, stated here once rather than in every branch:

  * The small integer types and the two floating-point types become `number`.
    A JavaScript `number` is an IEEE-754 double and holds every integer up to
    2^53 exactly, which covers TINYINT through INT, signed or unsigned, and
    YEAR. Width and signedness are therefore deliberately **not** expressed,
    and `column.unsigned` is read nowhere in this file.
  * BIGINT becomes `string`, and this is the one mapping that is a decision
    about the connection rather than only about the column. A BIGINT UNSIGNED
    reaches 2^64-1 and a `number` loses integer precision above 2^53, silently.
    Measured on this server: with mysql2's defaults `audit_event_id` arrives as
    `number`; with `supportBigNumbers` and `bigNumberStrings` both set it
    arrives as `string`. `connect` in `node/core.jinja` sets both, always, so
    the type here is `string` unconditionally. `supportBigNumbers` alone was
    rejected: it yields a `string` only when the value leaves the safe range,
    which is the type `number | string` — a type no static checker can help a
    caller with, and one whose shape depends on the data.
  * DECIMAL becomes `string`, which is what mysql2 returns and what loses
    nothing: a `number` cannot hold `decimal(38,10)`. Measured, and measured to
    bind back: `WHERE gross_weight_kg = ?` with the returned string matches the
    row it came from.
  * The BigInt primitive was rejected for both. mysql2 offers no option that
    produces one, so it would need a `typeCast` of this example's own, and a
    row carrying a BigInt throws on `JSON.stringify`, which is the one thing a
    caller of a Node data layer is most likely to do with a row.
  * TIME becomes `string`. Measured: `06:00:00` arrives as the string
    `"06:00:00"`. A MariaDB TIME is a signed interval of up to +/-838:59:59,
    which is not a time of day, and JavaScript has no interval type at all.
  * DATE, DATETIME and TIMESTAMP become `Date`, which is the only temporal type
    the language has. Measured, and the limit is measured too: mysql2 builds
    the `Date` in the connection's time zone, so a DATE of `2026-04-18` arrives
    as local midnight and prints as `2026-04-17T23:00:00.000Z` in a UTC+1
    process. The instant binds back unchanged — `WHERE payment_due_on = ?`
    matches — so the round trip is exact; what is inexact is reading a
    calendar date off the UTC fields of the result.
  * BIT becomes `Buffer`. Measured: `bit(8)` carrying 7 arrives as
    `<Buffer 07>`. The round-trip limit that follows is recorded in the
    example's README, and it is the same one the Python example found.
  * INET4, INET6 and UUID become `string`. Measured: the three arrive as
    character data — `203.0.113.24`, `2001:db8:3f2b::c7`, and a UUID as its 36
    characters of text rather than as 16 bytes.
  * The binary and blob types become `Buffer`.
  * The eight spatial types become `Buffer`, and that is a decision against
    what the driver does by default. Measured: mysql2 parses a geometry column
    into JavaScript objects — a POINT arrives as `{x, y}`, a LINESTRING as an
    array of those, a POLYGON as an array of rings — and those objects do not
    bind back: mysql2 serialises a plain object as a JSON string, so
    `WHERE reference_marker = ?` with the parsed point matched 0 rows, and an
    insert would store that JSON text. This example therefore asks the server
    for well-known binary and hands it back through a function, which is what
    `select_expression` and `bind` below are for, and that round trip was
    measured to match.
  * A JSON column is not a branch of its own because the model never reports
    one: MariaDB reports such a column as `longtext`, per FR-CAT-038.
-#}
{% macro value_type(column) -%}
{%- set kind = column | sql_type -%}
{%- if kind == "tinyint" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "smallint" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "mediumint" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "int" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "year" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "float" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "double" -%}number
{%- elif kind == "bigint" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "decimal" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "char" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "varchar" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "tinytext" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "text" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "mediumtext" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "longtext" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "enum" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "set" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "time" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "uuid" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "inet4" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "inet6" -%}string
{%- elif kind == "bit" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "binary" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "varbinary" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "tinyblob" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "blob" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "mediumblob" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "longblob" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "geometry" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "geometrycollection" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "linestring" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "multilinestring" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "multipoint" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "multipolygon" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "point" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "polygon" -%}Buffer
{%- elif kind == "date" -%}Date
{%- elif kind == "datetime" -%}Date
{%- elif kind == "timestamp" -%}Date
{%- else -%}
{{- fail("node/_types.jinja maps no JavaScript type onto the column " ~ column.table_name ~ "." ~ column.name ~ ", whose column_type is " ~ column.column_type ~ ". FR-EX-008 obliges this macro to cover every data_type the three schemas report: add a branch for it rather than letting an unmapped type reach tsc as a guess.") -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  field_type(column) — the type of one property of a row, nullability honoured.

  A nullable column is not the same JavaScript type as a non-nullable one, and
  `T | null` is where the language puts the difference: mysql2 returns `null`
  for a NULL, and `tsc --strict` makes a caller narrow the property before
  reading the value. `undefined` is deliberately not part of it: the property
  is always present on a row, and `undefined` is reserved for the filter, where
  it means *no condition*.
-#}
{% macro field_type(column) -%}
{%- if column is nullable -%}{{ value_type(column) }} | null
{%- else -%}{{ value_type(column) }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  spatial(column) — non-empty for the eight spatial types, empty otherwise.

  These are the columns whose value cannot travel as itself. `select_expression`
  and `bind` are the two places that consequence is spent, and this macro is the
  one place the membership is written down.
-#}
{% macro spatial(column) -%}
{%- set kind = column | sql_type -%}
{%- if kind in ["geometry", "geometrycollection", "linestring", "multilinestring",
                "multipoint", "multipolygon", "point", "polygon"] -%}yes
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  select_expression(column) — the column as a SELECT list names it.

  A spatial column is asked for as well-known binary, because the value mysql2
  decodes from the wire format cannot be bound back. Every other column is
  asked for as itself.

  The alias keeps the result's column names equal to the catalogue's, which
  costs nothing and makes a hand-run of one of these statements read the way
  the table does.
-#}
{% macro select_expression(column) -%}
{%- if spatial(column) -%}ST_AsWKB({{ sql_name(column.name) }}) AS {{ sql_name(column.name) }}
{%- else -%}{{ sql_name(column.name) }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  bind(column) — the placeholder that carries one value of this column.

  A spatial column's `Buffer` is well-known binary and MariaDB will not compare
  or store it as a binary string, so it is read back into a geometry by the
  server. Measured: `WHERE reference_marker = ST_GeomFromWKB(?)` matches the
  row the bytes came from, and `WHERE reference_marker = ?` with those same
  bytes matches nothing.

  Every other column binds as itself.
-#}
{% macro bind(column) -%}
{%- if spatial(column) -%}ST_GeomFromWKB(?)
{%- else -%}?
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  braced(text) — `text` wrapped in the braces a JSDoc type annotation takes.

  It exists because a template cannot write `{` immediately before `{{`: the
  lexer reads the pair as the start of an expression. Escaping the brace once
  here keeps every `@property` and `@param` tag in the templates readable.
-#}
{% macro braced(text) -%}
{{- "{" }}{{ text }}{{ "}" -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  sanitised(name) — a catalogue name with the punctuation JavaScript refuses in
  an identifier replaced by an underscore.

  This is needed because FR-ENV-031 passes a character outside `A-Za-z` through
  the naming filters unchanged: `pascal` renders the column
  `freight.legacy_edi_field.back`tick` as `Back`tick`, which is an identifier
  in no language. The underscore is chosen rather than removal because the
  naming filters then treat the position as a word boundary, per FR-ENV-030, so
  the column arrives as `backTick` rather than as `backtick`.

  The set below is the ASCII punctuation a quoted MariaDB identifier may carry.
  It is closed and it is not complete: a name beginning with a digit, and two
  names whose sanitised forms collide, still render code JavaScript refuses.
  That is deliberate — the compile gate of FR-EX-009 is the reader that reports
  it.

  A character outside ASCII is left alone, and that is a decision rather than an
  omission. JavaScript identifiers are defined over the Unicode ID_Start and
  ID_Continue properties of UAX #31, which `ç` and `ã` both satisfy, so
  `freight.legacy_edi_field.posição` is the property `posição` and is read as
  `row.posição`. Replacing the two letters would lose information for no gain,
  and there is no normalisation to worry about: the source of these files is
  UTF-8 and the identifier is compared by code point.
-#}
{% macro sanitised(name) -%}
{{- name | replace("`", "_") | replace('"', "_") | replace("'", "_")
         | replace("\\", "_") | replace("/", "_") | replace(".", "_")
         | replace(",", "_") | replace(";", "_") | replace(":", "_")
         | replace("?", "_") | replace("!", "_") | replace("*", "_")
         | replace("+", "_") | replace("=", "_") | replace("<", "_")
         | replace(">", "_") | replace("(", "_") | replace(")", "_")
         | replace("[", "_") | replace("]", "_") | replace("{", "_")
         | replace("}", "_") | replace("|", "_") | replace("^", "_")
         | replace("~", "_") | replace("%", "_") | replace("&", "_")
         | replace("#", "_") | replace("@", "_") | replace("$", "_")
         | replace("-", "_") | replace(" ", "_") -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  ident(name) — the JavaScript property name for a catalogue name: a property
  of a row, of a key, of a filter, and the name a repository takes on the
  aggregate.

  `camel` is the convention the language uses for a property, which is where
  this example differs from Python's `snake` and Go's exported `Pascal`. The
  difference is spelling and nothing else: all three read the same `name` field
  of the same model.

  **No keyword guard is needed, and that is a fact about JavaScript rather than
  an oversight.** Every name this macro produces is used as a property — in an
  object literal, in a `@property` tag, and in a member expression — and since
  ES5 a reserved word is a legal property name in all three positions.
  `freight.legacy_edi_field.select` is therefore the property `select`, read as
  `row.select`, where the Python example needed PEP 8's trailing underscore for
  its own keywords and Go needed none because `select` is not exported there.

  The one JavaScript name that would misbehave as a property is `__proto__`,
  which in an object literal sets the prototype instead of defining a property.
  It is out of reach: FR-ENV-030 discards an empty word, so a leading or
  trailing underscore cannot survive the word list, and a column called
  `__proto__` arrives here as `proto`.
-#}
{% macro ident(name) -%}
{{- sanitised(name) | camel -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  constant(name) — the name of one member of a table's column constants.

  UPPER_SNAKE is the convention for a constant, and it needs no guard for the
  same reason `ident` needs none: the member is a property of a frozen object.

  What it can produce is a name that keeps a non-ASCII letter's case, because
  FR-ENV-031 case-folds over `A-Za-z` alone: `posição` becomes `POSIçãO`. That
  is a legal JavaScript property name and it is what determinism costs, since a
  case-folding that knew about `ç` would be a locale-dependent one.
-#}
{% macro constant(name) -%}
{{- sanitised(name) | upper_snake -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  type_name(name) — the name of a type this example declares for a catalogue
  name: a row, a key, a filter, an ordering term, a repository.

  FR-ENV-033 records what `pascal` costs: an acronym loses its case, so
  `actor_id` becomes `ActorId` and not `ActorID`. The cost is accepted here
  rather than bought back with an initialism list, which would be a second
  opinion to maintain and a new way for two columns to collide on one property
  name.

  A type name declared by a `@typedef` is a name in the type space alone; it
  can shadow nothing at run time, and no JavaScript keyword can reach it,
  because `pascal` upper-cases the first letter of every word and every
  keyword is lower case.
-#}
{% macro type_name(name) -%}
{{- sanitised(name) | pascal -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  sql_name(name) — a catalogue name as a statement's text names it.

  `quote`, per FR-ENV-008 and FR-ENV-045, doubles a backtick inside the name
  and encloses the whole in backticks, which is what MariaDB parses back to
  exactly this name.

  Nothing further is needed, and that is worth saying because the Python
  example needed one more step. PyMySQL's paramstyle is `format` and builds the
  final statement as `query % arguments`, so a `%` in an identifier had to be
  doubled there. Every statement this example renders is executed through
  `Connection.execute`, which is a server-side prepared statement: the SQL
  reaches MariaDB unmodified and MariaDB counts the placeholders, so neither a
  `%` nor a `?` inside a quoted identifier is a hazard here. That is also why
  `Connection.query`, which interpolates client-side, is used nowhere in the
  rendered code.
-#}
{% macro sql_name(name) -%}
{{- name | quote -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  js_string(text) — a JavaScript string literal carrying arbitrary text.

  Emitted through the `json` filter of FR-ENV-036. A JSON string literal is a
  JavaScript string literal — the escapes JSON defines are a subset of
  JavaScript's — so a name carrying a quote, a backslash or a newline is
  escaped rather than ending the literal early.

  No rendered statement is built with a template literal, so a backtick inside
  a quoted identifier is inert in the source as well as correct in the SQL.
-#}
{% macro js_string(text) -%}
{{- text | json -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  jsdoc_text(text) — arbitrary catalogue text, made safe on one line of a
  `/** … */` block.

  Two transformations, and both are needed because a block comment is the one
  comment JavaScript has that its own content can close:

  * `*/` is broken with a space. A generated column's expression, a table
    comment and a column comment are catalogue text and may carry it, and the
    sequence would end the JSDoc block and put the rest of the text into the
    token stream.
  * a newline becomes a space. A tag's description continues onto the following
    lines until the next tag, so a catalogue line that happened to begin with
    `@` would be read as a tag.

  Catalogue text that must keep its shape — a table's own comment — is not
  passed through here. It is written as `//` line comments instead, through the
  `comment` filter of FR-ENV-038, because a line comment cannot be closed by
  its own content and a newline in it produces another line comment.
-#}
{% macro jsdoc_text(text) -%}
{{- text | replace("*/", "* /") | replace("\r", " ") | replace("\n", " ") -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  declared(column) — the one-line description of a column's declaration, for
  the `@property` tag that carries it.
-#}
{% macro declared(column) -%}
{%- set text -%}
`{{ column.name }}`, declared `{{ column.column_type }}` {{ "NULL" if column is nullable else "NOT NULL" }}
{%- if column is auto_increment %} AUTO_INCREMENT{% endif %}
{%- if column.invisible %} INVISIBLE{% endif %}
{%- if column.generated %} {{ column.generated.storage }} AS ({{ column.generated.expression }}){% endif %}.
{%- endset -%}
{{- jsdoc_text(text) -}}
{%- endmacro %}
